IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `mistake-domain`

§ `England and Wales — the settled common law, and the equity that may or may not have survived`

-- WHAT THIS MODULE IS FOR. England is where Singapore's common law limb comes
-- from, and it is also the jurisdiction against which Singapore defined
-- itself. Encoding it makes both halves of that visible: the common law rules
-- are the same rules, and the equitable limb is the one Singapore has and
-- England may not.
--
-- THE HONEST ANSWER IN THE MIDDLE OF THIS MODULE IS A REFUSAL. That is not an
-- encoding gap. Great Peace Shipping Ltd v Tsavliris Salvage (International)
-- Ltd [2003] QB 679 held that there is no equitable jurisdiction to rescind
-- for COMMON mistake, overruling Solle v Butcher [1950] 1 KB 671. What it did
-- to UNILATERAL mistake it did not decide, and the Singapore Court of Appeal,
-- surveying the point, could say only that two first-instance decisions "had
-- accepted that the equitable remedy of rescission for unilateral mistake
-- still survived Great Peace Shipping" (Chwee Kin Keong v Digilandmall.com
-- Pte Ltd [2005] SGCA 2 at [74], citing Huyton SA v Distribuidora
-- Internacional de Productos Agricolas SA de CV [2003] 2 Lloyd's Rep 780 and
-- Harrison v Halliwell Landau [2004] EWHC 1316). Two High Court decisions are
-- not a settled appellate rule. A model that answered anyway would be
-- inventing English law, and would be believed.

§§ `The common law limb — the same rule Singapore applies`

-- Smith v Hughes (1871) LR 6 QB 597, per Hannen J at 610, quoted in Chwee Kin
-- Keong at [32]: "If by any means he knows that there was no real agreement
-- between him and the promiser, he is not entitled to insist that the promise
-- shall be fulfilled in a sense to which the mind of the promiser did not
-- assent."
--
-- Hartog v Colin & Shields [1939] 3 All ER 566 is the locus classicus (Chwee
-- Kin Keong at [35]): hare skins offered at a price per pound instead of per
-- piece, "the court held that the apparent contract was void because the
-- plaintiff must have known and did, in fact, know that there was a mistake
-- in the offer". See also Shogun Finance Ltd v Hudson [2004] 1 AC 919 at
-- [123], cited at [31].
--
-- The elements are identical to Singapore's, which is the finding this module
-- exists to record: the divergence between the two jurisdictions is entirely
-- in equity, and nowhere in the common law.

@ref Hartog v Colin & Shields [1939] 3 All ER 566; Smith v Hughes (1871) LR 6 QB 597; as stated in Chwee Kin Keong at [31], [32] and [35]
GIVEN c IS A `Mistake case`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the contract is void at English common law` IF
        (CONSIDER c's `what the mistaken party was mistaken about`
         WHEN `a term of the contract` THEN TRUE
         OTHERWISE FALSE)
    AND c's `the mistake was fundamental`
    AND `the other party actually knew` c

§§ `Mere unilateral mistake, unknown to the other party — settled, and the contract stands`

-- Riverlate Properties Ltd v Paul [1975] Ch 133, as summarised in Chwee Kin
-- Keong at [70]: a lessor's solicitors omitted a repairing-contribution
-- clause; "[t]he English Court of Appeal held that as the error was purely that
-- of the lessor and/or its solicitors and the lessee and/or her solicitors
-- had no knowledge of such error and were not guilty of anything approaching
-- sharp practice, there were no grounds to intervene."
--
-- Russell LJ, at 141, quoted at [70]: "If reference be made to principles of
-- equity, it operates on conscience. If conscience is clear at the time of
-- the transaction, why should equity disrupt the transaction?"
--
-- And at 145, approving Redbridge London Borough Council v Robinson Rentals
-- Ltd (1969) 211 EG 1125, quoted at [71]: "mere unilateral mistake on the
-- part of the plaintiff, unknown to the defendant, was no ground for
-- rescission of the lease in question".
--
-- So the outer bound is settled even though the interior is not: with a clear
-- conscience on the other side, English law leaves the contract alone.

@ref Riverlate Properties Ltd v Paul [1975] Ch 133 at 141 and 145, as stated in Chwee Kin Keong at [70] and [71]
GIVEN c IS A `Mistake case`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the other party's conscience was clear` IF
        NOT (`the other party at least had reason to suspect` c)
    AND (CONSIDER c's `what the other party did`
         WHEN `nothing beyond accepting the bargain` THEN TRUE
         WHEN `carelessness alone`                   THEN TRUE
         OTHERWISE FALSE)

§§ `The gap Great Peace Shipping left`

-- THE REFUSAL IS NARROW, AND ITS NARROWNESS IS THE POINT. What Great Peace
-- Shipping unsettled is not "unilateral mistake in England" -- most of that
-- is settled -- but one class of case: the class on which SINGAPORE'S
-- equitable limb operates and its common law limb does not. Chwee Kin Keong
-- at [80] draws that class exactly: a fundamental mistake as to a term, the
-- other party with constructive but not actual knowledge, and an additional
-- element of impropriety. Singapore grants relief there. England, on the
-- authorities available, cannot be said either way.
--
-- Outside that class English law answers, and this module answers with it.
-- Smith v Hughes is the case that proves the limit matters: a KNOWN mistake
-- as to the quality of the oats rather than as to a term of the sale, and the
-- contract binding notwithstanding. A model that refused there would be
-- reporting doubt about a case decided in 1871.

@ref The class identified in Chwee Kin Keong at [80], on which English authority is divided after Great Peace Shipping
GIVEN c IS A `Mistake case`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the case falls in the class Great Peace Shipping left unsettled` IF
        NOT `the contract is void at English common law` c
    AND (CONSIDER c's `what the mistaken party was mistaken about`
         WHEN `a term of the contract` THEN TRUE
         OTHERWISE FALSE)
    AND c's `the mistake was fundamental`
    AND `the other party at least had reason to suspect` c
    AND (CONSIDER c's `what the other party did`
         WHEN `a conscious omission to disabuse`      THEN TRUE
         WHEN `deliberate steps to prevent discovery` THEN TRUE
         OTHERWISE FALSE)

-- The one refusal in this corpus. Its name is its message, per the house
-- pattern for a question the model cannot answer.

@ref Great Peace Shipping Ltd v Tsavliris Salvage (International) Ltd [2003] QB 679, and Chwee Kin Keong at [74] surveying its effect on unilateral mistake
GIVETH A `What becomes of the contract`
`whether equity in England still rescinds for unilateral mistake after Great Peace Shipping is unsettled` MEANS
    REFUSE "whether equity in England still rescinds for unilateral mistake after Great Peace Shipping is unsettled"

§§ `The verdict`

@export Decide what the law of England and Wales does with a contract one party entered into by mistake. Answers where English law is settled, and refuses on the one class of facts Great Peace Shipping left undecided.
GIVEN c IS A `Mistake case`   @desc The facts of the case, as found: what the mistake was about, whether it was fundamental, what the other party knew, and what he did about it.
GIVETH A `What becomes of the contract`
DECIDE `what English law does` c IS
    BRANCH
        IF `the contract is void at English common law` c THEN `the contract is void`
        IF `the case falls in the class Great Peace Shipping left unsettled` c
            THEN `whether equity in England still rescinds for unilateral mistake after Great Peace Shipping is unsettled`
        OTHERWISE `the contract stands`
